{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"1a1410e4609e6a61","name":"card_event_the_double_sunrise","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/ANZAC/events/card_event_the_double_sunrise","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"THE DOUBLE SUNRISE","text":"Give a random friendly unit on the battlefield and in hand +2+1.","flavorText":"The Double Sunrise was a wartime air service run by Qantas, Australia’s airline company, with RAAF personnel during WWII. It was created in 1943 to restore the air link between Australia and England after the fall of Singapore had cut the old route. The flights used Consolidated PBY Catalina flying boats between Western Australia and Lake Koggala in Ceylon (now Sri Lanka). The route flew over more than 3,500 nautical miles of the Indian Ocean and was then the longest nonstop airline service in the world. The flights were slow, lonely and difficult. Crews flew without radio navigation and relied on compass work and stars. Departures were timed so the aircraft crossed Japanese occupied areas in darkness. Westbound flights lasted between 27 and 33 hours, long enough for passengers and crew to see sunrise twice, which gave the service its name. The Catalinas carried heavy fuel loads, so each flight usually took only a few passengers and a small amount of urgent mail. Between 1943 and 1945, the service made 271 crossings, carried 860 passengers, and helped keep Australia connected to Britain.","faction":"Anzac","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_the_double_sunrise","title":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_the_double_sunrise_title","SourceString":"THE DOUBLE SUNRISE","LocalizedString":"THE DOUBLE SUNRISE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Anzac","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_the_double_sunrise_text","SourceString":"Give a random friendly unit on the battlefield and in hand +2+1.","LocalizedString":"Give a random friendly unit on the battlefield and in hand +2+1."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_the_double_sunrise_history","SourceString":"The Double Sunrise was a wartime air service run by Qantas, Australia’s airline company, with RAAF personnel during WWII. It was created in 1943 to restore the air link between Australia and England after the fall of Singapore had cut the old route. The flights used Consolidated PBY Catalina flying boats between Western Australia and Lake Koggala in Ceylon (now Sri Lanka). The route flew over more than 3,500 nautical miles of the Indian Ocean and was then the longest nonstop airline service in the world. The flights were slow, lonely and difficult. Crews flew without radio navigation and relied on compass work and stars. Departures were timed so the aircraft crossed Japanese occupied areas in darkness. Westbound flights lasted between 27 and 33 hours, long enough for passengers and crew to see sunrise twice, which gave the service its name. The Catalinas carried heavy fuel loads, so each flight usually took only a few passengers and a small amount of urgent mail. Between 1943 and 1945, the service made 271 crossings, carried 860 passengers, and helped keep Australia connected to Britain.","LocalizedString":"The Double Sunrise was a wartime air service run by Qantas, Australia’s airline company, with RAAF personnel during WWII. It was created in 1943 to restore the air link between Australia and England after the fall of Singapore had cut the old route. The flights used Consolidated PBY Catalina flying boats between Western Australia and Lake Koggala in Ceylon (now Sri Lanka). The route flew over more than 3,500 nautical miles of the Indian Ocean and was then the longest nonstop airline service in the world. The flights were slow, lonely and difficult. Crews flew without radio navigation and relied on compass work and stars. Departures were timed so the aircraft crossed Japanese occupied areas in darkness. Westbound flights lasted between 27 and 33 hours, long enough for passengers and crew to see sunrise twice, which gave the service its name. The Catalinas carried heavy fuel loads, so each flight usually took only a few passengers and a small amount of urgent mail. Between 1943 and 1945, the service made 271 crossings, carried 860 passengers, and helped keep Australia connected to Britain."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","isInPermanentPool":true,"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Organic_War_Drums_3.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/DOUBLE_SUNRISE.DOUBLE_SUNRISE","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_anzac","Key":"card_event_the_double_sunrise_photo","SourceString":"A Double Sunrise Catalina G-AGKS at Nedlands, Australia, 1943.","LocalizedString":"A Double Sunrise Catalina G-AGKS at Nedlands, Australia, 1943."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"Notes":"Image needed to be customized","isDefenseBuff":true},"variables":[{"name":"count","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"possibleCards","type":"TArray<UBaseCardObject*>","direction":"in"},{"name":"possibleUnits","type":"TArray<UBaseCardObject*>","direction":"in"},{"name":"tempCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_the_double_sunrise","owner":"card_event_the_double_sunrise_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"Temp_int_Array_Index_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_IsValid_ReturnValue","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counte